# AMD64 thunk export spec -- opengl32.dll.
#
# Read dlls/kernel32/kernel32.thunks for the trap ABI banner and
# dlls/vulkan-1/vulkan-1.thunks for the shape a whole-API module takes.  What
# is different here is that OPENGL32 IS THE ONE MODULE WHOSE GUEST SURFACE IS
# BIGGER THAN ITS EXPORT TABLE, and that difference is the entire design:
#
#   * Microsoft's opengl32.dll exports GL 1.1 and wgl* and nothing else, and
#     has since 1996.  Wine's opengl32.spec says the same 361 names, correctly.
#     Everything a modern game draws with is vended at RUNTIME by
#     wglGetProcAddress and appears in no export table on any Windows machine.
#     DOOM (2016)'s GL build carries a GLEW-style loader that asks for 2,497
#     names that way; 2,278 of them are ones Wine can answer.
#
#   * A guest calling wglGetProcAddress must receive a GUEST-CALLABLE stub, so
#     a stub for each of those names has to EXIST.  Hence FROM-SPEC names
#     opengl32-guest.spec rather than opengl32.spec: the .spec's 361 lines
#     copied verbatim (same names, same ordinals) plus one line per row of
#     extension_registry[] in thunks.c -- the table Wine's own
#     wglGetProcAddress answers from, so the guest surface is exactly what the
#     native module can serve and not one name more.  Both parts are
#     GENERATED, by ppc64le/opengl/gen-guest-surface.py, from wgl.h and
#     thunks.c; ppc64le/opengl/check-gl-smoke.sh fails if either output has
#     drifted from its inputs.
#
#   * ntdll resolves a trapping stub by looking its name up in the NATIVE
#     namesake's export table, and 2,753 of these names are not in it.  So
#     native opengl32 grows ONE private export, __wine_gl_entry_point (see
#     dlls/opengl32/wgl.c), which answers the same registry by name with no
#     context and no extension gating, and signal_ppc64.c's thunk_resolvers[]
#     names this module as the one that needs it.  The precedent is d3d12's
#     __wine_com_dispatch: one private entry point in the native module rather
#     than 2,753 exports nobody else wants.
#
#   * WHICH names the guest may actually have is still decided per call, by
#     the native wglGetProcAddress, inside signal_ppc64.c's
#     emu_wglGetProcAddress: it asks the native module first and only vends a
#     stub for a name the CURRENT context really offers.  A name Wine does not
#     know, or an extension this driver does not have, or an entry point the
#     oracle refused, all come back NULL -- once per name, loudly, by name.
#     NULL from wglGetProcAddress is an answer every GL loader already checks
#     for; a native ppc64 pointer is a crash none of them can diagnose.

DLL       opengl32.dll
FROM-SPEC opengl32-guest.spec

# The native module's private resolver entry point, kept out of the guest
# export list entirely: it is called by ntdll ON THE NATIVE MODULE, and a guest
# has no use for it and no business finding it.
EXCLUDE   __wine_gl_entry_point

# The 2,753 extension entry points are declared in Wine's headers only as
# PFN_* function-pointer typedefs, which the oracle -- reading FunctionDecls
# out of clang's AST -- cannot see.  opengl32_guest_ext.h restates each typedef
# as the declaration it implies, generated from the same wgl.h; wine/wgl.h
# itself carries the GL 1.1 prototypes and every GL type both files need.
PROBE-EXTRA wine/wgl.h
PROBE-EXTRA opengl32_guest_ext.h
# ...and six of the module's OWN .spec exports that Wine declares in no header
# either -- wglSwapBuffers and wglSetPixelFormat among them, without which a
# guest can neither choose a format nor present.  Hand-written; see the header.
PROBE-EXTRA opengl32_guest_wgl.h
INCLUDE-DIR .
